/*
 * Term glossary — what the word MEANS, in English and in Traditional Chinese.
 *
 * `wordparts.js` answers "what is this word built out of" and `term-notes.js`
 * answers "how do I say it". Neither of those is a definition, and a breakdown
 * without one is a party trick: being told that radioulnar reads as
 * "radius + ulna" does not tell you it names the two joints the forearm rotates
 * about. So every word the reading help can underline gets one plain line of
 * meaning here, and the Chinese term beside it.
 *
 * The Chinese is the point, not a decoration. This is a Hong Kong course taught
 * in English off English sources; a lot of this anatomy is already known under
 * its Chinese name, and carrying both halves lets one hook the other instead of
 * the same structure being learnt twice.
 *
 * PROVENANCE. Everything in this file is written by the app. It is standard
 * anatomical vocabulary rather than an invention, but it is NOT traced to a
 * file in the supplied source folders, and the term dialog says so in as many
 * words. The technical definition on the study item is the sourced one; this is
 * the line that gets you as far as reading it.
 *
 * Each entry is [meaning in English, 繁體中文]. Keys are lower case. Word-part
 * entries are keyed by the bare stem — `oste` covers oste/o, `itis` covers
 * -itis — because that is the form partOf() reduces a written part to.
 */

